\title{The Substrate-Independent Structure of Computation:\\
A Universal Complexity Hierarchy and the Diagonal Core of Impossibility}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We isolate the single mathematical fact that underlies every classical
obstruction in the theory of computation and show that, because it is a statement
of pure function theory, it is forced upon \emph{any} civilization capable of
expressing the notion of a function---independently of biology, physics, or
machine model. The fact is Lawvere's fixed-point theorem: if a type $A$ admits a
\emph{point-surjective} indexing $\varphi : A \to (A \to B)$ of its own $B$-valued
functions, then every endofunction $f : B \to B$ has a fixed point. From its
contrapositive we recover, uniformly, Cantor's theorem, the undecidability of the
halting problem, G\"odel's first incompleteness theorem, Tarski's undefinability
of truth, and Russell's paradox. We then prove \emph{substrate independence}:
every abstract computation model contains a decision behavior no program
realizes, with no hypotheses. We relativize this to an arbitrary oracle and prove
the \emph{hypercomputation barrier}: even granted an unrestricted, possibly
non-computable oracle, no oracle-program decides the model's own jump. We build
the \emph{universal complexity hierarchy}---the tower $\mathrm{Level}\,0 = A$,
$\mathrm{Level}\,(n{+}1) = (\mathrm{Level}\,n \to \mathrm{Bool})$---and prove it
strictly increases at every step and has no maximal level. Finally we exhibit the
positive face of the same theorem: Kleene's recursion theorem and quines in any
complete programming system, with the duality that a system complete for its own
program-transformations is never complete for its own Boolean decisions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring thesis in the philosophy of computation holds that any technological
civilization---regardless of its biological or physical substrate---must
rediscover the same structural obstructions to computation. Our aim is to give
this thesis a precise mathematical spine: to identify the exact statement
responsible for the obstructions, verify that it refers to no machine model or
physical resource, and derive from it the universal facts any computing
civilization must confront.

The organizing observation is that the classical impossibility
results---Cantor's theorem, Turing's undecidability of the halting problem,
G\"odel's first incompleteness theorem, Tarski's undefinability of truth, and
Russell's paradox---are instances of a single theorem of elementary function
theory: \textbf{Lawvere's fixed-point theorem}. Because that theorem speaks only
of types, functions, and fixed points, it is available to any civilization that
can express the notion of a function. This is the precise content of the slogan
``computational complexity is discovered, not invented.''

\section{Preliminaries}

We work in a standard type-theoretic setting. For types $A,B$ we write $A \to B$
for the type of functions from $A$ to $B$, $\mathrm{Bool} =
\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$ for the two-element type, and $!\,{\cdot}$ for
Boolean negation. A map $f:X\to Y$ is \emph{injective} if $f(x)=f(x')$ implies
$x=x'$ and \emph{surjective} if every $y$ equals some $f(x)$. We write $\lvert
X\rvert$ for cardinality; $2^{\kappa}$ is the cardinality of $X\to\mathrm{Bool}$
when $\lvert X\rvert=\kappa$.

\begin{lemma}[Negation is fixed-point free]\label{lem:neg}
For every $b:\mathrm{Bool}$, $b \neq \,!\,b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Check both cases: $\mathrm{true}\neq\mathrm{false}$ and
$\mathrm{false}\neq\mathrm{true}$.
\end{proof}

This single asymmetry is the only ``substrate input'' the theory requires.

\section{The diagonal core}

\begin{definition}[Point-surjectivity]
A map $\varphi : A \to (A \to B)$ is \textbf{point-surjective} if for every
$g : A \to B$ there is $a : A$ with $\varphi(a) = g$. Each code $a$ names an
answer-function $\varphi(a)$; point-surjectivity says every answer-function is
named---``the coding scheme is complete.''
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Lawvere's fixed-point theorem]\label{thm:lawvere}
If $\varphi : A \to (A \to B)$ is point-surjective, then every $f : B \to B$ has
a fixed point.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $d(x) = f(\varphi(x)(x))$. Choose $a$ with $\varphi(a) = d$. Then
$\varphi(a)(a) = d(a) = f(\varphi(a)(a))$, so $b := \varphi(a)(a)$ satisfies
$f(b)=b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract diagonal argument]\label{thm:diag}
If $f : B \to B$ has no fixed point, then no $\varphi : A \to (A \to B)$ is
point-surjective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor's theorem, Boolean form]\label{thm:cantor}
For every type $A$, no map $\varphi : A \to (A \to \mathrm{Bool})$ is surjective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A surjection is point-surjective, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:diag} the
fixed-point-free negation (Lemma~\ref{lem:neg}) could not exist. Explicitly,
$g(x)=\,!\,\varphi(x)(x)$ differs from every $\varphi(a)$ at $a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict Boolean-power step]\label{thm:step}
For every $A$ there is an injection $A \hookrightarrow (A \to \mathrm{Bool})$ but
no surjection $A \to (A \to \mathrm{Bool})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Send $a$ to its indicator $\mathbf{1}_a(x)=[x=a]$; if $\mathbf 1_a=\mathbf 1_b$,
evaluating at $b$ gives $b=a$. Non-surjectivity is Theorem~\ref{thm:cantor}.
\end{proof}

Every classical obstruction is Theorem~\ref{thm:diag} with a choice of $B$ and
fixed-point-free $f$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Result & $B$ & $f$ & $\varphi(a)(x)$ \\
\midrule
Cantor & $\mathrm{Bool}$ & negation & ``$x$ in set coded by $a$'' \\
Halting & $\mathrm{Bool}$ & negation & ``$a$ accepts $x$'' \\
G\"odel & sentences & prepend $\neg$ & ``$a$ codes a formula about $x$'' \\
Tarski & truth values & negation & truth predicate on codes \\
Russell & $\mathrm{Bool}$ & negation & set membership \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Substrate-independent uncomputability}

\begin{definition}[Computation model]
A \textbf{computation model} is a type $\mathrm{Pgm}$ of programs together with a
Boolean acceptance relation $\mathrm{acc}:\mathrm{Pgm}\to\mathrm{Pgm}\to
\mathrm{Bool}$, where $\mathrm{acc}(p)(q)$ reads ``$p$ accepts the code of $q$.''
No computability or structure is assumed.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Diagonal behavior]
$\mathrm{diag}(q) = \,!\,\mathrm{acc}(q)(q)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract undecidability]\label{thm:undec}
In every computation model, $\mathrm{acc}(p)\neq\mathrm{diag}$ for all $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\mathrm{acc}(p)=\mathrm{diag}$ evaluated at $p$ gives $\mathrm{acc}(p)(p)=
\,!\,\mathrm{acc}(p)(p)$, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:neg}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Substrate independence]
Every computation model has a decision behavior outside the range of
$\mathrm{acc}$, with no hypotheses.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take $\mathrm{diag}$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:undec}.
\end{proof}

\section{The hypercomputation barrier}

\begin{definition}[Oracle model]
An \textbf{oracle model} adds an arbitrary function
$\mathrm{oracle}:\mathrm{Pgm}\to\mathrm{Bool}$---a stand-in for an unrestricted,
possibly non-computable resource---to a computation model. Its \emph{jump} is
$\mathrm{jump}(q)=\,!\,\mathrm{acc}(q)(q)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hypercomputation barrier]\label{thm:hyper}
For every program type $\mathrm{Pgm}$, every oracle whatsoever, and every
acceptance relation, there is a decision behavior $g$ with
$\mathrm{acc}(p)\neq g$ for all $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $g=\mathrm{jump}$; the diagonal argument of Theorem~\ref{thm:undec} does not
use the oracle.
\end{proof}

Adding hypercomputational power does not dissolve the diagonal; it relocates it
one level up. The jump of a class is never internal to the class---the abstract
seed of relativization and the reason diagonalization alone cannot settle P vs
NP.

\section{The universal complexity hierarchy}

\begin{definition}[Level tower]
$\mathrm{Level}\,0 = A$ and $\mathrm{Level}\,(n{+}1) = (\mathrm{Level}\,n \to
\mathrm{Bool})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Strict step]\label{thm:strict}
For every $n$ there is an injection $\mathrm{Level}\,n\hookrightarrow
\mathrm{Level}\,(n{+}1)$ and no surjection onto it.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:step} with $X=\mathrm{Level}\,n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinal monotonicity and no maximum]\label{thm:card}
$\lvert\mathrm{Level}\,n\rvert<\lvert\mathrm{Level}\,(n{+}1)\rvert$ for all $n$;
hence $m<n$ implies $\lvert\mathrm{Level}\,m\rvert<\lvert\mathrm{Level}\,n\rvert$,
and for every $n$ there is $m>n$ with strictly larger cardinality. The hierarchy
has no top.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\lvert\mathrm{Level}\,(n{+}1)\rvert = 2^{\lvert\mathrm{Level}\,n\rvert} >
\lvert\mathrm{Level}\,n\rvert$ by Cantor's cardinal inequality; the sequence is
therefore strictly monotone.
\end{proof}

\section{The positive face: recursion, quines, duality}

\begin{definition}[Programming system]
A \textbf{programming system} is a type $\mathrm{Pgm}$ with a point-surjective
map $\mathrm{build}:\mathrm{Pgm}\to(\mathrm{Pgm}\to\mathrm{Pgm})$ indexing all
program-transformations---the abstract content of an acceptable programming
system.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Kleene's recursion theorem, abstract form]\label{thm:kleene}
In a programming system, every $f:\mathrm{Pgm}\to\mathrm{Pgm}$ has a fixed
program $e$ with $f(e)=e$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere} with $A=B=\mathrm{Pgm}$, $\varphi=\mathrm{build}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Quines]
For any $\mathrm{printer}:\mathrm{Pgm}\to\mathrm{Pgm}$ there is $e$ with
$\mathrm{printer}(e)=e$: a self-reproducing program.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No complete self-semantics]\label{thm:dual}
Even in a system complete for its transformations, for every
$\mathrm{sem}:\mathrm{Pgm}\to(\mathrm{Pgm}\to\mathrm{Bool})$ there is a behavior
$g$ with $\mathrm{sem}(p)\neq g$ for all $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $g(q)=\,!\,\mathrm{sem}(q)(q)$ and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:neg}.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:kleene} and~\ref{thm:dual} coexist: completeness for
transformations (answer type $\mathrm{Pgm}$) and incompleteness for decisions
(answer type $\mathrm{Bool}$) are both Lawvere's theorem with different answer
types. Creativity and limitation are two readings of one theorem.

\section{Applications and future work}

The results give an exact form to the universality thesis: any civilization able
to form the concept of a function from codes to answers possesses Lawvere's
theorem, and hence Cantor, the halting problem, the endless hierarchy, the oracle
barrier, and Kleene's recursion theorem. Future directions include: (i)
formalizing resource-bounded classes as closure operators on decision behaviors
and phrasing P-vs-NP-type separations as lattice properties, with the oracle
barrier as the seed of a Baker--Gill--Solovay relativization theorem; (ii)
instantiating the level tower with an explicit resource measure to obtain a
concrete time/space hierarchy theorem $\mathrm{DTIME}(f)\subsetneq
\mathrm{DTIME}(g)$ for $f=o(g/\log g)$; and (iii) recovering the non-degenerate
recursion theorem in the computable category via acceptable programming systems.

\section{Conclusion}

A single line of pure function theory organizes the entire landscape of
computational impossibility. Read backward it yields Cantor, the halting problem,
G\"odel's incompleteness, and an unbounded hierarchy; relativized it survives
arbitrary oracles; read forward it yields recursion and quines. None of these
mentions a machine, a bit, or a physical resource---they are theorems about the
shape of computation itself, forced on every possible computing civilization. The
mathematics of computation is the same across all worlds: discovered, not
invented.

